    "math_academy_id": "q-170638",
    "knowledge_point_id": "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a",
    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Linear Transformations",
    "problem": "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation",
    "worked_solution": "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(x+y) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{y}_{1})+({x}_{2}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true.",
