    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-170638: T is multiplication by the matrix [[1,1],[0,0]], so it preserves scalar multiplication and addition; all three statements hold. q-112876: a reduced rational root must have numerator dividing 7 and denominator dividing 14; 1/21 fails this necessary condition. q-113296: the common numerator is 7v+2(3-5v)=3(2-v), with v≠-3/2. q-14145: the distinct positive rational-root candidates are 1/10, 1/5, 2/5, 1/2, 4/5, 1, 8/5, 2, 4, and 8; including their negatives gives 20. q-112877: -2 has reduced numerator -2, which does not divide 9. These rational-root-theorem questions concern permissible candidates, not guarantees that candidates are actual roots. q-41576: the common numerator is 6y+5(9-4y)=45-14y, with y≠1/2. q-79551: the common numerator is 3t+2k, with k+3t≠0. q-66435: completing the square gives (x+8)^2=-100, hence p+q=-92 and x=-8±10i. q-113245: completing the square gives (x-3)^2=-25, hence p+q=-28 and x=3±5i. q-20851: (dy/dt)/(dx/dt)=12√t, giving 12 at t=1. q-6836: (-15 sin θ)/(12 cos θ)=-(5/4)tan θ wherever cos θ≠0. q-15465: the slope at t=π/2 is (1-sin t)/(1+cos t)=0/1=0. q-103910, q-103920, and q-103921: dividing by 28, 14, and 54 respectively gives the selected standard forms. q-70147: the common numerator is 2(1-x)+(3-x)=5-3x, with x≠1. No earlier submitted answers or grades were supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
